DON'T BE RICH

What Is Wealth For?

A Formal Theory of Civilizational Freedom of Purpose in the Age of Artificial Intelligence

Version 1.0 — Theoretical PhD Dissertation

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini

Abstract

Human civilization has historically organized a substantial part of its economic, social, and institutional architecture around scarcity, labor, production, income, and wealth accumulation. Artificial intelligence and advanced robotics may progressively weaken the historical relationship between human labor, economic production, and material security.

The central problem is therefore not simply whether automation will eliminate jobs, nor whether societies should adopt universal basic income, shorter working weeks, or alternative economic systems. A deeper civilizational question emerges: What happens to human purpose when technological civilization progressively reduces the necessity of human labor?

Existing approaches frequently attempt to answer this question by proposing a preferred replacement: prosperity, happiness, leisure, human flourishing, social equality, capabilities, creativity, community, ecological sustainability, or another conception of the good life. This research proposes a different starting point. Rather than determining what humanity should ultimately live for, the theory asks: How can civilization preserve humanity's capacity to choose, revise, and pursue diverse conceptions of a meaningful life without allowing any single political ideology, economic system, institution, corporation, religious framework, or artificial intelligence to define that purpose for everyone?

We call this principle Civilizational Freedom of Purpose. The preliminary thesis is that a mature civilization should not maximize one predetermined conception of human flourishing. It should maximize the durable space of legitimate human possibilities, subject to fundamental constraints such as human agency, material security, social participation, ecological continuity, protection against coercive domination, and distributed control over the infrastructure of cognition and production.

The provocative title Don't Be Rich therefore does not mean that wealth is undesirable. It asks a more fundamental question: What is wealth for? Wealth, technology, intelligence, energy, computation, and automation may be understood as instruments that expand civilization's capacity to act. They should not automatically become the terminal objectives of civilization. The ultimate object of study is therefore not wealth itself, but the relationship between civilizational capacity and human purpose.

Version 1.0 note: This revision integrates sustained critical review, deep internet search (2023–2026), and the final theoretical synthesis. The principal changes are: (1) full integration of the post-work paradox as a structural legitimacy threat; (2) formalization of temporal authorship and standing legitimacy as distinct from event-based legitimacy; (3) incorporation of techno-feudalism literature on compute concentration and the dissolution of labor-capital boundaries; (4) specification of the Sovereign Algorithmic Wealth Fund and source-based levies on AI intellectual property rents; (5) material preconditions for the right to fork, including publicly funded compute infrastructure; (6) engagement with non-Western critiques of autonomy and pluralism from Confucian relational personhood and Islamic karamah; (7) empirical evidence from basic income experiments on mental health and purpose; (8) political economy analysis of transition-phase countervailing power; (9) direct measurement challenges for ℛ(Ω_H); and (10) acknowledgment of performative diversity risks in the multi-LLM protocol.

