16. Falsifiability

The theory must be designed so that it can fail. Since this is a theoretical PhD without experiments or simulations, falsification pathways must be defined as theoretical conditions — conditions under which the theory would be logically refuted or rendered incoherent — testable through conceptual analysis and comparative historical evidence.

· F1 — Purpose Collapse. Demonstrate formally that any institutional architecture satisfying C necessarily reduces ℛ(Ω_H) below a threshold of meaningful diversity over time.
· F2 — Coordination Failure. Prove that a civilization cannot maintain sufficient collective action capacity without a shared substantive conception of the good, under any institutional arrangement satisfying C.
· F3 — Agency–Stability Tradeoff. Show that increasing ℛ(Ω_H) necessarily produces unacceptable reductions in governance stability, where "unacceptable" is defined by a specified threshold.
· F4 — Institutional Capture. Demonstrate that the institutions intended to preserve purpose pluralism inevitably become ideological authorities themselves.
· F5 — AI Substitution. Demonstrate that sufficiently advanced AI systems necessarily require centralized purpose determination for safe coordination.
· F6 — Ownership Capture. Demonstrate that any institutional arrangement compatible with C will inevitably concentrate control over cognitive and productive infrastructure, rendering Layer VII unachievable.
· F7 — Symbolic Participation Capture. Demonstrate that any institutional arrangement satisfying C will inevitably produce symbolic rather than consequential participation, rendering Layer IV functionally vacuous and confirming the post-work paradox as an inescapable feature of advanced automation.
· F8 — Compute Irreversibility. Demonstrate that compute concentration is a stable equilibrium: once a threshold of concentration is reached, no institutional arrangement compatible with C can reverse it, rendering Layer VII unachievable.
· F9 — Coordination Collapse Under Pluralism. Demonstrate that polycentric governance systems satisfying P (pluralism) inevitably fail to coordinate on defense, ecological management, or infrastructure, confirming F2 through the specific mechanism of polycentric complexity.
· F10 — Transition Lock-In. Demonstrate that the transition period creates path dependencies that determine the mature post-work order, such that institutional choices during transition are more consequential than mature-state design — and that transition-period dynamics systematically favor concentration over distribution.
· F11 — Temporal Domination Capture. Demonstrate that standing legitimacy conditions are either impossible to institutionalize or inevitably collapse into event-based proceduralism, rendering T8 unachievable.

These are not objections to be avoided. They are experimental targets — theoretical conditions whose demonstration would refute or substantially weaken the theory.

