17. Research Methodology

The project should combine:

A. Philosophical Analysis. Examine existing theories of liberal neutrality, perfectionism, human flourishing, capabilities, post-work society, political legitimacy, autonomy, republican freedom, pluralism, future generations, the neutrality–perfectionism debate, techno-feudalism and concentrated power, and non-domination as a design principle for AI. Engage with non-Western traditions of relational personhood and dignity.

B. Economic Analysis. Study automation, labor displacement, productivity, capital ownership, distribution, post-work economics, alternative measures of development, concentration of AI infrastructure, rentier dynamics in cognitive capitalism, and fiscal instruments for the transition (robot taxes, sovereign wealth funds, token taxes, pre-distribution, source-based levies on AI intellectual property rents).

C. Institutional Analysis. Study constitutions, democratic systems, social insurance, education, labor institutions, governance adaptation, institutional failure, anti-monopoly and anti-concentration frameworks, the right to fork as a constitutional principle, standing legitimacy conditions for AI governance, and public compute infrastructure.

D. Comparative Historical Analysis. Study pre-industrial aristocratic and clerical classes, modern Nordic and residual welfare experiments, intentional communities and religious orders that deliberately decoupled livelihood from market employment, post-disaster or post-conflict societies where normal economic structures temporarily collapsed, and historical attempts to create non-employment-based participation and their outcomes. Use basic income experiments as quasi-experimental evidence on material security without purpose architecture.

E. Formal Analysis of Institutional Equilibria. Since this is a theoretical PhD without code training or simulation, formal analysis replaces agent-based modeling. Draw on game-theoretic models of coordination and pluralism, theoretical literature on institutional equilibria under heterogeneous value functions, logical analysis of the consistency and sufficiency of the constraint set C, and theoretical conditions for preservation of Ω_H across generations. The simulation literature may be cited as motivation, but the claims themselves must be established through conceptual and mathematical argument.

F. AI-Assisted Adversarial Research. Use multiple independent AI systems to search for prior art, conceptual overlaps, hidden assumptions, mathematical contradictions, ideological bias, counterexamples, alternative formulations, and stronger competing theories.

Transparency requirement. Each participating model must document its known training-data provenance, alignment methodology, and disclosed political-philosophical biases. The human director must publish full adversarial transcripts, including moments where models disagree about the normative foundations of the theory itself.

Critical qualification on performative diversity. The protocol's value is limited by the training-data convergence of frontier models. All major LLMs are trained on overlapping corpora, optimized by similar RLHF procedures, and deployed within similar institutional constraints. Their disagreements may be less substantive than they appear. Research on LLM bias confirms that models inherit biases from training data and alignment processes, influencing their responses in subtle ways, and that bias persistence and flexibility vary across linguistic and cultural contexts. Including models trained on non-Western corpora or explicitly designed for adversarial reasoning would enhance the protocol's value. Without this, the protocol risks producing the illusion of adversarial review.

