D.5 Non-Western Engagement: Confucian and Islamic Perspectives

D.5.1 Confucian Relational Personhood

Confucian philosophy holds that persons are constituted by their relationships and social roles. The individual is not an autonomous chooser who exists prior to their relationships; rather, the individual becomes a person through ethical commitments and social responsibilities. As the Confucian tradition emphasizes, individuals are made distinctively human by their ability to conceive of interests not purely in terms of personal self-interest, but in terms that include a relational and a communal self.

This poses a challenge to the constraint set C, which is formulated in terms of individual autonomy and pluralism. If autonomy is understood individualistically — as the capacity to choose one's own life project independent of social relationships — then Confucianism may reject it. But if autonomy is reconstructed relationally — as the capacity to sustain relationships of mutual recognition, contribution, and care without subjection to arbitrary power — then C is compatible with Confucianism.

The theory proposes the latter reconstruction. Constraints A (agency), P (pluralism), and D (distributed control) can be reformulated as conditions under which persons can sustain relationships of mutual recognition and contribution without subjection to domination. This is a substantive theoretical task, but it is not an insurmountable obstacle.

D.5.2 Islamic Karāmah

Islamic ethics grounds human dignity (karāmah) in divine sovereignty rather than individual choice. Human dignity is intrinsic and remains intact regardless of circumstance, status, or productivity. Core Islamic ethical principles include:

· Tawḥīd (divine unity): the foundational principle that God is one, which grounds all ethical obligations.
· Khilāfah (stewardship): humans are trustees of God's creation, not owners.
· Amānah (trust): human life is a trust from God rather than personal possession.
· ʿAdl (justice): the requirement to act justly in all dealings.
· Maṣlaḥah (public welfare): the pursuit of collective welfare.
· Karāmah insāniyyah (human dignity): the intrinsic dignity of every human being.

Islamic ethics permits beneficial AI applications while imposing firm constraints where technologies undermine justice, human agency, dignity, or public welfare.

The theory's constraint set C is compatible with Islamic ethics if C is understood as specifying the conditions under which karāmah can be realized and sustained. Constraint S (material security) is required for the realization of dignity in material existence. Constraint A (agency) is required for moral responsibility (taklif). Constraint P (pluralism) is compatible with Islamic ethics if it is understood as protecting the space for legitimate religious and cultural diversity, not as endorsing religious pluralism in the theological sense. Constraint D (distributed control) is compatible with the Islamic principle that God is the ultimate owner and humans are trustees.

D.5.3 The Reconstruction of Autonomy as Relational Non-Domination

The theory proposes that the conditions of autonomy can be reconstructed as conditions of relational non-domination. This means:

· Positive condition: Persons must be able to sustain relationships of mutual recognition, contribution, and care.
· Negative condition: Persons must not be subjected to arbitrary power in those relationships.
· Structural condition: The infrastructure of cognition and production must not systematically bias relationships toward domination.

This reconstruction is compatible with both Confucian relational personhood and Islamic karāmah. It is thinner than liberal individualism (which prioritizes the autonomous chooser) but thicker than procedural neutrality (which takes no position on the quality of relationships).

