D.6 Empirical Evidence from Basic Income Experiments

D.6.1 Finnish Basic Income Experiment

The Finnish basic income experiment (2017–2018) randomized 2,000 unemployed individuals to receive a partial basic income of €560 per month, with a control group of approximately 173,000. The study found:

· Mental health: Participants in the intervention group had moderately better mental health compared with those in the control group (adjusted risk difference for poor mental health −0.08, 95% CI: −0.12; −0.03).
· Heterogeneity: Multilevel modelling and causal forest showed no evidence for heterogeneous effects on mental health across age, gender, education, prior employment status, household size, and family type.
· Psychotropic drug use: Decreased by 8–11% among intervention group participants.
· Employment: No significant reduction in labor supply.

D.6.2 German Basic Income Study

The German three-year basic income study (2020–2023) randomized participants to receive €1,200 per month. The study documented:

· Mental health: Improvements of 0.347 standard deviations.
· Purpose in life: Improvements of 0.250 SD.
· Life satisfaction: Improvements of 0.417 SD.
· Stress levels: Reductions in perceived stress.
· Labor market: No withdrawal from the labor market — participants did not work fewer hours on average.
· Persistence: Effects persisted 18 months after the program ended.

D.6.3 Minneapolis Basic Income Experiment

The Minneapolis basic income experiment (2024) found:

· Food security: Improvements.
· Housing stability: Improvements.
· Financial security: Improvements.
· Labor supply: No effect.
· Mental health: Improvements in mental health and self-reported well-being while participants were receiving payments.

D.6.4 Implications for H2

These findings support the theory's H2 refinement:

· Material security without purpose architecture is insufficient — the studies do not show that basic income alone produces flourishing; they show that it improves mental health and well-being, but the effect sizes are moderate (0.25–0.42 SD).
· Material security with structured commitment produces measurable well-being gains — the improvements in purpose in life (0.250 SD) suggest that when material security is combined with structured activity (as in the German study, where participants were encouraged to pursue meaningful projects), purpose outcomes improve.
· The "social hammock" myth is not supported — none of the studies found that basic income reduced labor supply or increased dependency.

The theory's H2 should therefore be read as specifying that the decoupling of material security from employment creates a new institutional requirement for purpose architecture, but the effect of automation on meaning is ambivalent: AI can expand Ω_H when it creates new achievement opportunities and contract it when it renders human contribution superfluous.

